K Berrien <nospamme@nospam.com> wrote:
> 
> 7.2 RH would be ok.  Hope it doesn't stink big time like the i386
> versoins did.

There is a very substantial difference between 7.2 on x86 and 7.2-Alpha.
The later is actually closer to "standard" 7.3 although it is still
different.  Make sure that all updates are installed.  I know that a
kernel based on one of Red Hat 2.4.18 kernel (or maybe this will end up
with 2.4.20?) is "in works" and it should show up RSN but when really it
is hard to tell.  No that this can stop you from doing something like
that on your own. :-)

> How much difference is there between the alpha processors.

Depends on what you are looking at. :-)  The main difference between
various Alpha models is machine with a PCI bus, which are supported
by Linux, and those with Turbochannel, which are not.  If NT was
running on that hardware then it will be not a Turbochannel.
Also current distributions really are geared towards SRM firmware
which is totally alien to NT.  Various boards do not have SRM
available and getting something like 7.2-Alpha on these, although
possible, will be not obvious or easy.  There are _technical_
advantages to running from SRM but sometimes you cannot do this
even if both kinds of firmware are available (like a hardware
from which you need to boot but recognized only by one firmware
and not another).  Capabilities of a firmware, even with the
same name, may vary drastically from a board to board and a version
to version.

> Really
> helpful to compile kernels, main apps per your CPU model/revision?

glibc comes in variants compiled for various processor families.  If you
are spending so much time in kernel to worry about Alpha processor
specific optimizations then you have much more serious problems.  OTOH
recompiling your kernel for a specific configuration will free some
additional memory for your user space.  How much of gain is that depends
on your local conditions. You will pay with an extra time required to
maintain your customized setup.  As for applications the answer is "it
depends".  For example SSL can possibly take an advantage of byte
operations (probably more if you would be able to rewrite inner loops in
assembler like on x86 - hard as you have to think about scheduling
issues :-) but you have to have a substantial load to really notice that
in "real life".  Easier way is to use Compaq compilers for your
applications.  For that you either have to qualify for an "enthusiast
license", and accept limitations spelled out there, or buy these.
Libraries used by these compilers work also with gcc and may provide
most of the gain.  Once again "it depends".

  Michal


