The one from http://www.suse.de/~stepan/ (2.2.18) works.
I know because just two days ago I was installing Red Hat 7.2-Alpha
on ruffian.  As for ldmilo I do not even know if there was ever more
than one.  The one I used prints "[JAY'S HACKED VERSION]".

> rh 6.2 is the only linux that the working milo can load,
> i have no idea why.

This was explained in this newsgroup many times.  Basically milos expect
from a kernel a specific load address which kills other machines.
Although I would think that RH 7.1 with a default configuration should
boot but I do not know that for sure.  Yes, that means that I needed a
custom kernel for that 7.2 installation and that the whole process was
"interesting". :-)  OTOH after it was installed it runs nice.  An
ultimately used kernel evolved from a Red Hat 2.14.18-27.7.x.  Some
minor imperfections but none of show stoppers.  In particular rebooting
takes OS down and does not go any further.  It is possible that using
"Ruffian" kernel instead of a "generic" one would solve that but I had
no time to try.  Likely wrong expectations (SRM only) from firmware.

If you do not have already some earlier distro running on Ruffian then
probably the easiest way to do that install is to take a disk to another
Linux machine (does not have to be Alpha at all), partition and format
that properly (in particular a FAT-type partition table, a few megs FAT
partition for ldmilo and milo, and a small _ext2_ partition for / with a
file system laid with 'mke2fs -b 1024 -O none ...'), drop on that some
basic system with a kernel which boots your Ruffian, i.e., among other
things, a compiled in support for your disks and CDs, and continue from
there.  There are other ways but this seems to be a simple one for a
"general case".  Ruffian has an ev56 processor so do not install
glibc for anything higher or your user-space will die immediately.

   Michal


