Hi,

The ZX is close to a LX164. The CF7 jumper (on the jumper pack located
between the two EFI proprietary bus slots) will allow you to start the
Fail Safe Bootloader. If you connect a floppy you can get the  Debug
Monitor (don't remember the DEC link to it ...) to boot and eventually
flash the bootrom. Starting from there Debian will boot and install like
a charm. The on-board PCI SCSI chip (sym53c875) is on a secondary PCI
bus, that leads to some `interesting' behavior with MILO. I never tried
SRM on a ZX.

Generally the informations from LX164 would apply to ZX.

Of course, that kind of operation will void your warranty right away...

[ When I developed the bootrom/firmware/OS for the ZX I hadn't yet managed
  to get EFI to consider Linux. ]

<disclaimer>
I didn't release any proprietary information that could damage EFI IP.
</disclaimer>

